Bug: a file under a -prune'd directory is accepted as part of the project

The config prunes sub/. The scan honours it - from a clean state sub/pruned.c is
never scanned - but -getproject for sub/pruned.c answers set-info for the
project. Expected is that the file is outside the project.

Cause: scanning and membership use different definitions of the project.
scanProjectStructure() (src/server.c) passes resolvePrunePaths() to the walk;
handleProject() (src/cxref.c) only tests the root prefix and the -I directories
and never looks at -prune.

A consequence seen while writing this: -getproject registers its file in the
file table, and the table is written to .c-xref/db. After one run the snapshot
holds sub/pruned.c, and the next start loads it ("'.../sub/pruned.c' scanned:
added" from cxfile.c) as if the project owned it. The same registration is what
test_getproject_unknown_cu_under_include_path is suspended on.

Likely fix: one definition of "own" - root plus listed directories, minus
-prune - used by both the scan and the membership answer (ADR-0031, Proposed).

Decided (ADR-0031): the file is outside, and the answer says no project was found
for it. Discovery from sub/pruned.c finds the open project's own root, so a
switch would restart into the same project; the client must not offer one.
Expected here is today's project-mismatch, since the protocol has no form for
"outside, no project found" yet. Change expected when that form exists.
